The thing about language is that one wants to be clear.
The "rules" help us to be clear.  It's OK to break the rules
if it aids clarity, and that's sort of the point (and the
fun) of English, and why it's won ascendency as the the
most powerful language in the world.  We can break our
rules and still be clear or even more clear.  Breaking rules
in other languages often makes the sentence unintelligible.

Bach laid down rules for writing music, and then broke those
same rules all time.  That principle applies in writing also.
If you want to talk about your wand's charges and you think 
that it makes your sentence run more smoothly than talking
about the charges of your wand, then I'm 100% for your
choice.

Writing isn't about what you can get away with, but about
getting your ideas across clearly.  If your prose reads like
stereo instructions, then don't get mad at your reader for
misunderstanding you or for giving up.

